    "obs_and_metrics": [],
    "innovation": "This paper demonstrates that a machine learning model can diagnose accurate precipitation fields from a small set of easily assimilated large-scale atmospheric variables, potentially bypassing the need for traditional microphysical and convective parameterizations.",
    "kg_extraction_triplets": [
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "ERA5",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "INHERITED_FROM",
            "target_entity_instance": "IFS",
            "target_entity_type": "Source",
            "paragraph": "The approximations used to parameterize these processes, combined with the lack of assimilated observations in the total precipitation field yield known biases in the IFS forecasts as well as in the popular reanalysis dataset ERA5, which uses the IFS as its base model.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "IFS",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "USES_PHYSICAL_SCHEME",
            "target_entity_instance": "convective parameterization",
            "target_entity_type": "PhysicalScheme",
            "paragraph": "Traditional global NWP models like the European Center for Medium Range Weather Forecast- ing\u2019s (ECMWF) Integrated Forecasting System (IFS) generate precipitation through two parame- terizations, one representing sub-grid-scale deep convection and the other microphysical processes occurring in response to adiabatic cooling during grid-resolved vertical motions (ECMWF 2024).",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "IFS",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "USES_PHYSICAL_SCHEME",
            "target_entity_instance": "mass-flux scheme",
            "target_entity_type": "PhysicalScheme",
            "paragraph": "The convective parameterization estimates vertical transport of heat and mois- ture at the subgrid level. In the IFS this is implemented with a mass-flux scheme triggered in unstable conditions and tuned using empirically derived estimates of entrainment and detrainment (ECMWF 2024).",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "MLERA5",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "PRODUCES_VARIABLE",
            "target_entity_instance": "precipitation",
            "target_entity_type": "Variable",
            "paragraph": "In this section, we analyze the global performance of a model (MLERA5 ) trained to reproduce ERA5 3-hr precipitation from the 13 input fields in Table 1. This allows us to assess the degree to which the ERA5 precipitation fields can be determined by a small collection of easy-to-assimilate fields in contrast to a more conventional combination of microphysical and deep convective pa- rameterizations.",
            "ontology_conformed": false,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "MLIMERG",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "PRODUCES_VARIABLE",
            "target_entity_instance": "precipitation",
            "target_entity_type": "Variable",
            "paragraph": "We now evaluate a similar model (MLIMERG ) except that it is trained with IMERG data as the target. Directly training against observational data has the potential to improve precipitation forecasts beyond those obtained via more traditional parameterizations, although we currently lack a perfect dataset against which to train.",
            "ontology_conformed": false,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "ERA5",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "PRODUCES_VARIABLE",
            "target_entity_instance": "precipitation",
            "target_entity_type": "Variable",
            "paragraph": "Precipitation in the ERA5 dataset is obtained primarily from short-term model forecasts by ECMWF\u2019s IFS as part of the data assimilation process (some radar data from over the US is also assimilated). IFS precipitation is calculated using deterministic parameterizations of microphysical processes and deep convection.",
            "ontology_conformed": false,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "IMERG",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "PRODUCES_VARIABLE",
            "target_entity_instance": "precipitation",
            "target_entity_type": "Variable",
            "paragraph": "Satellite observations are from NASA\u2019s Integrated Multi-satellitE Retrievals for GPM (IMERG) version 7 dataset, which includes precipitation fields every 30 minutes from 2000 to present. IMERG combines microwave, infrared, and rain gauge estimates of precipitation to provide a \u2018best estimate\u2019 record of precipitation at the surface (Huffman et al. 2020).",
            "ontology_conformed": false,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "MLERA5",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "HAS_PHYSICAL_FEATURE",
            "target_entity_instance": "1\u00b0\u00d71\u00b0 latitude-longitude global mesh",
            "target_entity_type": "PhysicalFeature",
            "paragraph": "Our focus will be on resolutions in space and time appropriate for typical climate model simulations: a 1\u00b0\u00d71\u00b0 latitude- longitude global mesh and 3- or 24-h accumulations.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": false,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "MLIMERG",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "HAS_PHYSICAL_FEATURE",
            "target_entity_instance": "1\u00b0\u00d71\u00b0 latitude-longitude global mesh",
            "target_entity_type": "PhysicalFeature",
            "paragraph": "Our focus will be on resolutions in space and time appropriate for typical climate model simulations: a 1\u00b0\u00d71\u00b0 latitude- longitude global mesh and 3- or 24-h accumulations.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "MLERA5",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "HAS_METRIC",
            "target_entity_instance": "FSS",
            "target_entity_type": "Metric",
            "paragraph": "Figure 4 gives several globally averaged skill metrics for MLERA5 evaluated against ERA5 pre- cipitation in the test set. Figure 4a shows the fraction skill score across six intensity categories for a neighborhood size of 5\u00d75 grid cells. Data is not plotted for the most frequent intensity category of dry or almost dry (precipitation less than 1 mm d\u22121). For thresholds below 10 mm d\u22121, FSS values exceed 0.9 and approach a perfect score of 1.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "MLIMERG",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "HAS_METRIC",
            "target_entity_instance": "FSS",
            "target_entity_type": "Metric",
            "paragraph": "Further quantitative comparison of MLIMERG with ERA5 is presented in Fig. 6, which shows metrics spatially averaged between 70\u00b0 N and 70\u00b0 S over the 2022\u20132024 test set. Figure 6a shows the fraction skill score computed over 5\u00d75 grid-cell neighborhoods, binned by threshold intensities. The FSS diagnosed from MLIMERG is better (higher) than that from ERA5 at all intensity levels.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "ERA5",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "HAS_METRIC",
            "target_entity_instance": "FSS",
            "target_entity_type": "Metric",
            "paragraph": "In contrast to ERA5, MLIMERG scores above the nominal threshold for skillful forecasts at all intensities, whereas ERA5 falls short at the lowest intensity of 1\u20132 mm d-1.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": false,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "MLERA5",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "HAS_METRIC",
            "target_entity_instance": "ETS",
            "target_entity_type": "Metric",
            "paragraph": "The equitable threat score (ETS) for MLERA5 3-h and 24-h accumulations is given in Fig. 4b.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "MLIMERG",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "HAS_METRIC",
            "target_entity_instance": "ETS",
            "target_entity_type": "Metric",
            "paragraph": "Figure 6b compares Equitable Threat Scores (ETS) as a function intensity for 24-h accumulations. MLIMERG demonstrates higher skill than ERA5 at all intensity thresholds, indicating more reliable detection of events beyond random chance.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "ERA5",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "HAS_METRIC",
            "target_entity_instance": "ETS",
            "target_entity_type": "Metric",
            "paragraph": "Figure 6b compares Equitable Threat Scores (ETS) as a function intensity for 24-h accumulations. MLIMERG demonstrates higher skill than ERA5 at all intensity thresholds, indicating more reliable detection of events beyond random chance.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "MLERA5",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "HAS_METRIC",
            "target_entity_instance": "1-SEEPS",
            "target_entity_type": "Metric",
            "paragraph": "The 1-SEEPS score in Fig. 4c gives a measure of overall performance on dry, light, and heavy categories, as determined from the climatology in each grid cell. The MLERA5 scores are approxi- mately 0.94, which are both quite close to a perfect score of 1 and also well above the 0.72 value determined for a \u201cperfect-model\u201d forecast at 80-km grid spacing (Rodwell et al. 2010).",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "MLIMERG",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "HAS_METRIC",
            "target_entity_instance": "1-SEEPS",
            "target_entity_type": "Metric",
            "paragraph": "Lastly, Fig. 6c shows that the 1-SEEPS score for MLIMERG again exceeds ERA5 in skill, con- sistently yielding better estimates of dry, light, and heavy precipitation conditions.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "ERA5",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "HAS_METRIC",
            "target_entity_instance": "1-SEEPS",
            "target_entity_type": "Metric",
            "paragraph": "Lastly, Fig. 6c shows that the 1-SEEPS score for MLIMERG again exceeds ERA5 in skill, con- sistently yielding better estimates of dry, light, and heavy precipitation conditions. Note that the 1-SEEPS values for MLIMERG exceed those for the \u201cperfect model\u201d comparison of (G\u00f6ber 2008), while the ERA5 results are worse.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
